# Ledgerly environment variables
# ------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env.local` (local development) or paste the values into
# your host's dashboard (Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables).
#
# Every variable below is documented with: what it does, whether it is required,
# its default when unset, and an example. Only DATABASE_URL (or POSTGRES_URL) is
# needed to run the app; everything else is optional.
#
# Note: alert *rules* (thresholds, schedule, channels, custom Telegram wording)
# are NOT environment variables — they live in the `app_settings` table and are
# edited on the /alerts page. Only the Telegram and Web Push credentials are
# read from the environment, and even those can be set in the UI instead.

# ─── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# DATABASE_URL
#   What:  PostgreSQL connection string used by every query (src/db/index.ts).
#   Need:  Required at runtime. Not needed for `next build` (the pool is lazy).
#   Form:  postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE
#   Notes: Remote hosts must use TLS. `sslmode` may be disable/require/verify-ca/
#          verify-full/prefer — other URL options (certificate file paths) are
#          rejected. On Vercel a localhost host is refused.
#   Local: postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/app_db
#   Vercel/Neon/Supabase: use the provider's *pooled* connection string.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/app_db

# POSTGRES_URL
#   What:  Fallback connection string, read only when DATABASE_URL is unset.
#   Need:  Optional. Set automatically by the Vercel Postgres / Neon integration.
#   Notes: DATABASE_URL always wins when both are present.
# POSTGRES_URL=

# ─── Administrator access ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# DATABASE_SETUP_TOKEN
#   What:  Administrator key that protects the database setup wizard
#          (/api/database) and database export/import.
#   Need:  Optional. Without it those endpoints are open, like the rest of the
#          app — set it before exposing Ledgerly to the internet.
#   Form:  Random secret of at least 32 characters. Shorter values are ignored
#          (treated as "not configured").
#   Notes: Never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that would ship it to the browser.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# DATABASE_SETUP_TOKEN=

# ALLOW_DATABASE_SETUP_WRITE
#   What:  Lets the setup wizard save the connection string to `.env.local`.
#   Need:  Optional. Development already allows saving; this is for a persistent
#          self-hosted production server. Always disabled on Vercel.
#   Form:  true | false (anything else = disabled)
# ALLOW_DATABASE_SETUP_WRITE=true

# ─── Scheduled jobs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# CRON_SECRET
#   What:  Shared secret for /api/cron/refresh-prices and
#          /api/cron/daily-snapshot.
#   Need:  Optional but recommended. When set, callers must send
#          `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>` and wrong callers get 401.
#          When unset, the endpoints stay open and each route self-throttles to
#          one run per minute (per server instance).
#   Form:  Any long random string, identical in the scheduler's config.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# CRON_SECRET=

# ─── Telegram alerts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials can also be entered on /alerts → Telegram bot, where they are
# stored in `app_settings` (and win over these variables). Use the variables
# when you would rather keep secrets out of the database.

# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
#   What:  Bot token from @BotFather, used to send alert messages.
#   Need:  Optional. Required together with TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID to deliver alerts.
#   Form:  <digits>:<35+ characters>  e.g. 123456789:AAExampleTokenFromBotFather
#   Notes: The token is a secret. It is never returned to the browser and never
#          written to the alert log.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID
#   What:  Where the bot sends messages.
#   Need:  Optional. Only used when TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is valid.
#   Form:  Numeric user id (123456789), group id (-1001234567890), or
#          @channelname. The bot must already have received a message from you
#          (or post in that group/channel).
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# ─── Web Push (browser / installed web clip) ────────────────────────────────

# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY + VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY
#   What:  Identifies this server to browser push services (web-push).
#   Need:  Optional. Leave BOTH unset and a key pair is generated once and stored
#          in `app_settings` — alert setup then works with no configuration.
#   Form:  Base64url key pair from `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`.
#   Notes: Set both or neither; a half-configured pair is ignored. Changing or
#          clearing the database makes each browser re-subscribe.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# VAPID_SUBJECT
#   What:  Contact address sent to push services (they may use it to reach you).
#   Need:  Optional. Defaults to mailto:ledgerly@localhost.
#   Form:  mailto:you@example.com or https://example.com  (other values ignored)
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ─── Set by the platform — do not define these yourself ─────────────────────

# VERCEL
#   What:  Presence means "running on Vercel": the pool drops to one connection,
#          remote TLS is required, local hosts are refused, and saving
#          `.env.local` is disabled.
#   Need:  Optional. Injected by Vercel; setting it locally would make the app
#          behave as if it were deployed there.

# NODE_ENV
#   What:  "development" enables the database setup write path and React's dev
#          checks; "production" is set by `next start` and by Vercel.
#   Need:  Optional. Managed by Next.js — leave it alone.
